soc: mediatek: mt6797-a72: give the cluster a cpufreq policy

The two A72s came up and stayed at one clock and one voltage for the rest of
the boot: 2002 MHz at 1180 mV. That is the worst of both worlds. 1180 mV is
the vendor's voltage for 2145 MHz, two operating points ABOVE the clock
actually in use, and it was held whether the cores were working or idle -- so
the two cores with no governor were also the two drawing the most power at
rest.

It matters more than tidiness. This pack measures 312 mOhm internal resistance
(105 discharge samples on 2026-08-27: 660 mA at 3867 mV against 1221 mA at
3691 mV), so every extra amp costs 310 mV of headroom. With cpu8/cpu9 pinned at
maximum voltage the device browned out 71 seconds after being unplugged, twice,
hard, with no shutdown sequence; with them offline it ran for minutes. This is
the same observation as #59.

The OPP table is the vendor's own for cluster B, transcribed in
04-docs/A72-OPP-TABLE-2026-08-24.md from ubports-3.18
drivers/misc/mediatek/base/power/mt6797/mt_cpufreq.c. It is the FY1221 /
CPU_LEVEL_0 bin -- the lowest-clocked of the six the vendor selects between on
two efuse fields -- because mainline mt6797 has no nvmem node and the efuse
cannot be read. On a faster-binned die that under-clocks and can never
over-volt.

The table is deliberately cut at 1989 MHz rather than the vendor's 2145.
1989 MHz at 1140 mV is 0.65% slower than the 2002 MHz this driver has been
using and 40 mV below it, so every point is at or better than the status quo on
both axes. Raising the ceiling is a separate decision with a separate risk and
should not ride along with this.

Voltage ordering is asymmetric and deliberate: the rail rises before the clock
and falls after it. Getting that backwards hangs the cluster, which on this
board is a watchdog reset. The DA9214 encoding is confirmed against mainline
drivers/regulator/da9211-regulator.{c,h} (MIN_MV 300, STEP_MV 10, VBUCK_MASK
0x7F, BUCK_SL 0x80), matching what the existing bring-up path already writes.

The fixed raise in a72_raise_fn now stands down when cpufreq is live, so the
governor and the driver do not both write ARMCAXPLL2.
---
--- a/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-a72.c
+++ b/drivers/soc/mediatek/mt6797-a72.c
@@ -57,6 +57,7 @@
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/sysfs.h>
+#include <linux/cpufreq.h>
 #include <linux/kobject.h>
 
 /* System Power Manager: the SoC-internal power switches. */
@@ -185,6 +186,9 @@
 static void __iomem *topckgen_base;
 static bool a72_ready;
 static bool vproc2_on;
+/* set once cpufreq_register_driver() succeeds; read by a72_raise_fn above the
+ * cpufreq block, which is why it is declared here rather than beside it. */
+static bool a72_cpufreq_live;
 
 static int da9214_write(struct i2c_adapter *ad, u8 reg, u8 val)
 {
@@ -460,6 +464,14 @@
 
 static void a72_raise_fn(struct work_struct *w)
 {
+	/*
+	 * With a governor attached this is the governor's job, and doing it
+	 * here as well would mean two writers racing on the same PLL. The
+	 * fixed raise remains only as the fallback for a kernel where the
+	 * cpufreq driver failed to register.
+	 */
+	if (a72_cpufreq_live)
+		return;
 	mt6797_a72_retune(A72_RUN_MHZ_DEFAULT);
 }
 
@@ -482,6 +494,224 @@
 
 static struct kobj_attribute a72_freq_attr = __ATTR_RW(freq_mhz);
 
+/* ---- cpufreq ------------------------------------------------------------
+ *
+ * Until now this cluster came up and sat at one clock and one voltage for the
+ * rest of the boot: A72_RUN_MHZ_DEFAULT at VPROC2_MV, 2002 MHz at 1180 mV.
+ * That is the worst of both worlds. 1180 mV is the vendor's voltage for
+ * 2145 MHz, two operating points ABOVE the clock actually being used, and it
+ * is held whether the cores are working or idle -- so the two cores with no
+ * governor were also the two drawing the most power at rest.
+ *
+ * It matters more than tidiness. Measured 2026-08-27, this pack has an
+ * internal resistance of 312 mOhm (105 discharge samples, 660 mA at 3867 mV
+ * against 1221 mA at 3691 mV). At that resistance every extra amp costs
+ * 310 mV of headroom, and with these two cores pinned at maximum voltage the
+ * device browned out 71 seconds after being unplugged -- twice, hard, with no
+ * shutdown sequence. With cpu8/cpu9 offline it ran for minutes. Giving the
+ * cluster a governor is what makes the machine usable on battery, and #59's
+ * "eight cores at maximum takes the machine down" is the same observation.
+ *
+ * OPP TABLE SOURCE: the vendor's own, cluster B, transcribed in
+ * 04-docs/A72-OPP-TABLE-2026-08-24.md from
+ * ubports-3.18 drivers/misc/mediatek/base/power/mt6797/mt_cpufreq.c. The
+ * vendor's frequencies are kHz and its voltages are in units of 10 uV despite
+ * a "/ * 10MV * /" comment in that file: 118000 x 10 uV = 1.18 V, which is the
+ * rail this driver already brings up. So opp-kHz = vendor, mV = vendor / 100.
+ *
+ * WHICH BIN: the vendor selects one of six tables from two efuse fields
+ * (FUNC_CODE / DATE_CODE). We cannot read the efuse -- mainline mt6797 has no
+ * nvmem node and /sys/bus/nvmem/devices is empty -- so this is FY1221,
+ * CPU_LEVEL_0, the LOWEST-clocked of the six. On a faster-binned die that
+ * under-clocks; it can never over-volt. FIXME: read the efuse once an
+ * mtk-efuse node exists and pick the real bin.
+ *
+ * CEILING: the table is deliberately cut at 1989 MHz rather than the vendor's
+ * 2145. 1989 MHz at 1140 mV is 0.65% slower than the 2002 MHz this driver has
+ * been running and 40 mV BELOW it, so every point here is at or better than
+ * the status quo on both axes. Raising the ceiling is a separate decision with
+ * a separate risk, and #59 is still open; this change should not smuggle it in.
+ */
+struct a72_opp {
+	unsigned int khz;
+	unsigned int mv;
+};
+
+static const struct a72_opp a72_opps[] = {
+	{ 1989000, 1140 },
+	{ 1963000, 1130 },
+	{ 1872000, 1110 },
+	{ 1690000, 1070 },
+	{ 1599000, 1050 },
+	{ 1404000, 1000 },
+	{ 1287000,  980 },
+	{ 1170000,  950 },
+	{ 1053000,  930 },
+	{  936000,  900 },
+	{  793000,  880 },
+	{  650000,  850 },
+	{  338000,  800 },
+};
+
+static struct cpufreq_frequency_table a72_freq_table[ARRAY_SIZE(a72_opps) + 1];
+static unsigned int a72_cur_mv = VPROC2_MV;
+
+/*
+ * Set VPROC2 (DA9214 BUCKB, the A72 core rail) to an exact millivolt.
+ *
+ * Encoding confirmed against mainline drivers/regulator/da9211-regulator.{c,h}
+ * for this part: DA9211_MIN_MV 300, DA9211_STEP_MV 10, DA9211_VBUCK_MASK 0x7F,
+ * DA9211_BUCK_SL 0x80. So code = (mV - 300) / 10, and bit 7 is the slew-select
+ * the existing bring-up path already sets. Callers pass only voltages that
+ * appear in a72_opps[], so no clamping table is invented here.
+ */
+static int a72_set_vproc2_mv(unsigned int mv)
+{
+	struct i2c_adapter *ad;
+	struct device_node *np;
+	u8 code;
+	int r;
+
+	if (mv < 300 || mv > 1570)		/* da9211-regulator.c MIN/MAX_MV */
+		return -EINVAL;
+	code = (mv - 300) / 10;
+
+	np = of_find_node_by_path(DA9214_I2C_ALIAS);
+	if (!np) {
+		pr_err("no \"%s\" alias; cannot set VPROC2\n", DA9214_I2C_ALIAS);
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+	ad = of_get_i2c_adapter_by_node(np);
+	of_node_put(np);
+	if (!ad) {
+		pr_err("i2c6 adapter unavailable; cannot set VPROC2\n");
+		return -ENODEV;
+	}
+
+	r = da9214_write(ad, DA9214_PAGE_CON, 0);
+	if (!r)
+		r = da9214_write(ad, DA9214_VBUCKB_A, code | 0x80);
+	i2c_put_adapter(ad);
+
+	if (r) {
+		pr_err("VPROC2 set to %u mV failed (%d)\n", mv, r);
+		return r;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The rail needs time to settle before the cores are asked to run
+	 * faster on it. The bring-up path above waits 20 ms after enabling the
+	 * buck; a step between adjacent OPPs is far smaller, but this is the
+	 * one delay in the sequence that protects against raising the clock
+	 * onto a rail that has not arrived. FIXME: no DA9214 slew figure is
+	 * cited anywhere in this tree, so 2 ms is a conservative guess rather
+	 * than a datasheet number -- verify and replace.
+	 */
+	usleep_range(2000, 2500);
+	a72_cur_mv = mv;
+	pr_debug("VPROC2 now %u mV\n", mv);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static int a72_cpufreq_target(struct cpufreq_policy *policy, unsigned int index)
+{
+	unsigned int khz = a72_freq_table[index].frequency;
+	unsigned int mv = a72_opps[index].mv;
+	unsigned int old_mv = a72_cur_mv;
+	int ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * ORDER MATTERS AND IS NOT SYMMETRIC. Going up, the rail must arrive
+	 * before the clock does or the cores run faster than their voltage
+	 * supports. Coming down, the clock must drop first for the same reason
+	 * in reverse. Getting this backwards does not misbehave gracefully --
+	 * it hangs the cluster, and on this board a hung cluster is a watchdog
+	 * reset.
+	 */
+	if (mv > old_mv) {
+		ret = a72_set_vproc2_mv(mv);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
+		ret = mt6797_a72_retune(khz / 1000);
+		if (ret) {
+			pr_err("retune to %u kHz failed (%d); backing the rail out to %u mV\n",
+			       khz, ret, old_mv);
+			a72_set_vproc2_mv(old_mv);
+			return ret;
+		}
+	} else {
+		ret = mt6797_a72_retune(khz / 1000);
+		if (ret) {
+			pr_err("retune to %u kHz failed (%d); leaving the rail at %u mV\n",
+			       khz, ret, old_mv);
+			return ret;
+		}
+		if (mv < old_mv) {
+			ret = a72_set_vproc2_mv(mv);
+			if (ret)	/* clock is already low; high rail is safe */
+				pr_warn("clock is at %u kHz but the rail is still %u mV (%d)\n",
+					khz, old_mv, ret);
+		}
+	}
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static unsigned int a72_cpufreq_get(unsigned int cpu)
+{
+	return a72_cur_mhz * 1000;
+}
+
+static int a72_cpufreq_init(struct cpufreq_policy *policy)
+{
+	int cpu, i;
+
+	if (MPIDR_AFFINITY_LEVEL(cpu_logical_map(policy->cpu), 1) !=
+	    MPIDR_CLUSTER_A72)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
+	/*
+	 * Both A72s share ARMCAXPLL2 and the VPROC2 rail, so they are one
+	 * policy. Deriving the mask from MPIDR rather than hardcoding 8 and 9
+	 * keeps this correct if the topology is ever renumbered.
+	 */
+	for_each_possible_cpu(cpu)
+		if (MPIDR_AFFINITY_LEVEL(cpu_logical_map(cpu), 1) ==
+		    MPIDR_CLUSTER_A72)
+			cpumask_set_cpu(cpu, policy->cpus);
+
+	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(a72_opps); i++) {
+		a72_freq_table[i].driver_data = i;
+		a72_freq_table[i].frequency = a72_opps[i].khz;
+	}
+	a72_freq_table[i].frequency = CPUFREQ_TABLE_END;
+	policy->freq_table = a72_freq_table;
+
+	/*
+	 * The firmware's power-on gate brings the cluster up at A72_CLK_KHZ,
+	 * which is not a table entry, so the driver's idea of the current
+	 * frequency would not match any OPP. Land on the lowest point
+	 * explicitly and let the governor take it from there.
+	 */
+	policy->cur = a72_cur_mhz * 1000;
+	policy->cpuinfo.transition_latency = 300 * NSEC_PER_USEC;
+
+	pr_info("cpufreq: policy for cpu%*pbl, %zu OPPs, %u-%u MHz\n",
+		cpumask_pr_args(policy->cpus), ARRAY_SIZE(a72_opps),
+		a72_opps[ARRAY_SIZE(a72_opps) - 1].khz / 1000,
+		a72_opps[0].khz / 1000);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static struct cpufreq_driver a72_cpufreq_driver = {
+	.name		= "mt6797-a72",
+	.flags		= CPUFREQ_NEED_INITIAL_FREQ_CHECK,
+	.verify		= cpufreq_generic_frequency_table_verify,
+	.target_index	= a72_cpufreq_target,
+	.get		= a72_cpufreq_get,
+	.init		= a72_cpufreq_init,
+	.attr		= cpufreq_generic_attr,
+};
+
 /* Runs on the controlling CPU, immediately before the target is brought up. */
 static int mt6797_a72_prepare(unsigned int cpu)
 {
@@ -611,7 +841,25 @@
 		pr_warn("no sysfs knob; the cluster is fixed at its default clock\n");
 
 	a72_ready = true;
-	pr_info("registered; cpu8/cpu9 can be onlined (the rail comes up on demand)\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * Registering here creates no policy: cpu8/cpu9 are still offline and
+	 * cpufreq only builds a policy for an online CPU. The policy appears
+	 * when the cluster is onlined, which is also when the rail exists.
+	 * A failure is not fatal -- the cluster still runs, at the fixed clock
+	 * a72_raise_fn falls back to -- but it must be loud, because a silently
+	 * ungoverned pair of A72s at maximum voltage is what this change exists
+	 * to end.
+	 */
+	ret = cpufreq_register_driver(&a72_cpufreq_driver);
+	if (ret)
+		pr_err("cpufreq driver did not register (%d); the cluster will run at a fixed %u MHz and %u mV\n",
+		       ret, A72_RUN_MHZ_DEFAULT, VPROC2_MV);
+	else
+		a72_cpufreq_live = true;
+
+	pr_info("registered; cpu8/cpu9 can be onlined (the rail comes up on demand)%s\n",
+		a72_cpufreq_live ? ", cpufreq active" : "");
 	return 0;
 
 err:
